WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:08.000
Counter-Strike: Global Offensive is by
definition a first-person shooter. That

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.480
means that incredible aim is at the core
of what makes the game so exciting.

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:24.960
(Casting)

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:30.460
But at the top level, strategy is incredibly important too.
Having the right tactics for a map can

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:32.400
be the difference between victory or
defeat.

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:47.980
(Casting)

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:49.560
And map mastery comes in the form

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:53.760
of strategies, often ones that rely on
utility. That means grenades,

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.050
flashbangs, molotovs and smokes. All
designed to force enemies out of

00:00:58.050 --> 00:00:59.880
position and deny them sightlines.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:31.300
(Casting)

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:35.180
But sometimes a player will do
something that subverts expectations, and

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:37.780
no player embodies that more than s1mple.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:51.840
In Counter-Strike there are individuals
that have made names for themselves by

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:58.289
defying conventions. For example, in a 1v1
against kennyS at ESEA LAN 13, North

00:01:58.289 --> 00:02:02.360
American CS prodigy Swag threw his
pistol to fake a flashbang.

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:06.780
kenny turned away from the impending
blast that never came, and he cleaned up

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:08.140
kenny for the clutch.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:42.740
(Casting)

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:44.560
North American CS pro

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:49.660
Stewie2K also famously disregards
expectations of the game. Instead of

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:53.680
cautiously playing around the smoke
like most players, he aggressively pushes

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:57.280
through it. Often catching players who
expect him to be cautious off guard.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:35.040
(Casting)

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:37.880
But s1mple, known for his confidence and the skills to back it up,

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:39.970
is one of the game's most exciting players.

00:03:39.970 --> 00:04:02.460
(Casting)

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:07.270
"There is just a little edge of
something different about s1mple that

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:11.820
makes you think, I actually believe this guy can do anything in the game."

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:17.080
And s1mple, like every great, refuses to accept the limitations of the expectations put upon him

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:19.900
by others. And that's led to some
incredible highlights.

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:31.740
(Casting)

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:35.560
At ESL One New York 2016  —
a tournament s1mple and Na`Vi would go on

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:39.440
to win — he made a fateful decision in a
1v1 against Team Liquid's nitr0.

00:04:39.440 --> 00:05:07.520
(Casting)

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:11.320
In a shocking move, s1mple
threw the A-W-P — a sniper rifle worth

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:16.640
$4750 — over the wall into Dust2's B bomb site.

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:20.420
"Sometimes you will see someone throwing their pistol to pretend it's

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:24.800
a grenade. To have people turn around, but
what did he think it was going be an

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:28.850
airstrike coming in with a missile from
the sky? What is s1mple actually doing."

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:33.020
The exact effect this had on the 1v1
is still a matter of debate. It wouldn't

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:36.170
be unreasonable for a player in Nitro's
position to be distracted by the

00:05:36.170 --> 00:05:40.820
incoming flying object. Then again he
didn't seem to react too dramatically to

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:44.990
it. But throwing the world's most
expensive flashbang is perfectly in line

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:50.680
with s1mple's audacious nature. He was
preying on others' expectations, and won the duel because of it.

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:52.960
"It was just like fate you know, he's like what the

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:54.140
f*** is going on."

00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:59.440
Of course, given the
sheer flashiness of the play, there were

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:04.520
plenty of people who wanted to recreate
it. To varying degrees of success.

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:28.940
And of course, it wasn't the first time
somebody threw an AWP. But it was the

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:31.580
first time it happened on a stage of
this size.

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:37.040
"I think that on the pro level, Tier 1 level, I think that
the fake flash thing works one in maybe

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:41.760
a fifty, or a hundred times even. So him
throwing his gun to do it is just a

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:45.090
more of a psychological thing than
anything else, because think of how much

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:46.780
that's insult to injury to nitr0."

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:49.400
As long
as there's an established way to play CS

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:52.860
there will always be cunning opponents
who exploit these patterns.

00:06:53.800 --> 00:07:12.360
(Casting)

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:14.220
And in October 2016,

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:18.320
s1mple reminded us why those
expectations can be a liability — and how

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:23.100
they can let the AWP double as a $4,750
flashbang.

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:32.420
(Casting)

